    "q-223782": {
      "dom_id": "step-q223782",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14165969/assets/0c18987fb16fc67df39e9887f152b2c6f38649b58e4bf469c80feb908eded8d2.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(2,2).$\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $6.$ This occurs at the point $(-2,6).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $2$ and $6,$ but not including $2$ nor $6.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $2<y<6,$ which can also be written as $2<f(x)<6.$",
